<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Santa Cruz de Mar Pequeña</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1478</span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Santa Cruz de Mar Pequeña was a fortified trading post founded on the Atlantic coast of north-west <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> in 1478 by Diego García de Herrera, lord of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canary-islands">Canary Islands</a>. It traded with local <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berber</a> tribes and was abandoned in the early 16th century. In the 19th century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> claimed its site, which was identified with Ifni.</p></div>
